ie's deposition and exile from Wallachia in February 1545."}, {"sourceSlug": "radu-paisie", "sourceName": "Radu Paisie", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "vlad-vintila-de-la-slatina", "targetName": "Vlad Vintilă de la Slatina", "context": "Radu Paisie succeeded Vlad Vintilă de la Slatina as Prince of Wallachia in June 1535 following the latter's assassination."}], "places": [{"name": "Târgoviște, Wallachia", "role": "Princely Capital"}, {"name": "Istanbul, Ottoman Empire", "role": "Ottoman Suzerain Seat"}, {"name": "Cairo, Egypt", "role": "Place of Exile and Death"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Radu Paisie", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "radu-paisie"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2621323", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radu_Paisie"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Radu Paisie's decade-long reign as Prince of Wallachia was regionally significant for solidifying the principality's vassal status under the Ottoman Empire and setting a precedent for direct Ottoman intervention in local succession. His rule contributed to the long-term geopolitical reorientation of the Danubian principalities.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-30T07:07:38Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30521406406", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Radu VII Paisie, officially Radul voievod(a) (Church Slavonic: Радул воєвода; Greek: Ῥαδουλ-Βοδα, romanized: Radul-Voda), also known as Radu vodă Măjescul, Radu vodă Călugărul, Petru I, and Petru de la Argeș (c. 1500 – after 1545), was Prince of Wallachia almost continuously from June 1535 to February 1545. A man of uncertain origins, he depicted himself as an heir to the House of Basarab and the Drăculești: the son of Prince Radu the Great and half-brother of Vlad Vintilă and Radu of Afumați. The scholar Nicolaus Olahus partly supported this account and further claimed that Paisie was his own cousin.", "newValue": "Radu VII Paisie, also recognized by epithets such as Radu vodă Măjescul and Petru de la Argeș, served as Prince of Wallachia almost continuously from June 1535 to February 1545. His reign was a precarious balancing act amidst the escalating influence of the Ottoman Empire, a period marked by intense internal dynastic struggles and the constant threat of annexation. Paisie, despite his uncertain origins, strategically presented himself as a legitimate heir to the prestigious House of Basarab and the formidable Drăculești lineage, claiming descent from Radu the Great to solidify his claim to the throne of this vital Danubian principality.

His ascension followed the turbulent reign of Vlad Vintilă de la Slatina, whom Paisie himself may have helped depose, marking a shift in the delicate power dynamics between Wallachian boyars and Ottoman suzerainty. During his decade-long rule, Radu Paisie navigated complex diplomatic terrain, often paying significant tribute to the Ottoman Porte to maintain a semblance of autonomy, including a reported annual payment of 15,000 gold ducats. He faced numerous challenges, including the rebellion of Șerban of Izvorani in 1536 and the constant machinations of rival claimants, many supported by the Ottomans, such as Barbu Craiovescu and his own half-brother, Mircea Ciobanul.

Ultimately, Radu Paisie's reign underscored Wallachia's diminishing sovereignty, transforming the principality into an increasingly reliable Ottoman vassal state rather than a buffer zone. His eventual deposition by Sultan Suleiman the Magnificent in February 1545, in favor of Mircea Ciobanul, and subsequent exile to Egypt, cemented the trend of direct Ottoman intervention in Wallachian succession. This shift had profound implications for the region, accelerating the Ottomanization of the Balkans and the Danubian principalities."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-30T07:07:38Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30521406406", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The escalating power of the Ottoman Empire necessitated Wallachian rulers to secure Ottoman recognition for their claim to the throne.", "Internal dynastic struggles within the House of Basarab created a volatile political landscape ripe for new claimants like Radu Paisie.", "The deposition of his predecessor, Vlad Vintilă de la Slatina, opened a power vacuum that Radu Paisie exploited with Ottoman backing."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-30T07:07:38Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30521406406", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1545"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1545"}], "newValue": ["Radu Paisie's reign further solidified Wallachia's status as a vassal state of the Ottoman Empire, increasing its financial obligations.", "His deposition and exile established a precedent for direct Ottoman intervention in Wallachian succession,